ENFORCEMENT DECREE OF THE ACT ON SUPPORT FOR VOLUNTARY ACTIVITIES OF ENTERPRISES FOR DISASTER MITIGATION

CHAPTER I GENERAL PROVISIONS

Article 1 (Purpose)
The purpose of this Decree is to prescribe matters delegated by the Act on Support for Voluntary Activities of Enterprises for Disaster Mitigation and matters necessary for its enforcement.

CHAPTER II DISASTER MANAGEMENT STANDARDS

Article 2 (Timing and Procedures for Investigation, Analysis, and Evaluation of Disaster Mitigation Activity Plans)
(1) The Minister of the Interior and Safety shall conduct investigations, analyses, and evaluations of enterprises' disaster mitigation activity plans, etc. pursuant to Article 6 (1) of the Act on Support for Voluntary Activities of Enterprises for Disaster Mitigation (hereinafter referred to as the "Act") (hereinafter referred to as "investigations") in any of the following cases: <Amended on Jan. 19, 2016; Nov. 29, 2016; Jul. 26, 2017>
1. Where amendment of disaster management standards is necessary because statutes or regulations related to disaster management are enacted or amended, or international standards are established or amended;
2. Where it is deemed necessary to strengthen enterprises' disaster mitigation activities, etc. due to the occurrence of a large-scale disaster or similar circumstances;
3. Other cases where the Minister of the Interior and Safety deems an investigation of enterprises' disaster mitigation activity plans, etc. necessary.
(2) Investigations shall, in principle, be conducted through documentary examination; provided, an on-site investigation may be conducted where on-site confirmation is necessary.
(3) When conducting an investigation, the Minister of the Interior and Safety shall notify the enterprises subject to the investigation in advance, in writing, of the subjects and content of the investigation and other relevant matters. <Amended on Jan. 19, 2016; Nov. 29, 2016; Jul. 26, 2017>
(4) Details necessary for the methods and procedures of investigations and related matters, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) through (3), shall be determined and publicly notified by the Minister of the Interior and Safety. <Amended on Jan. 19, 2016; Nov. 29, 2016; Jul. 26, 2017>

Article 3 Deleted. <Jul. 9, 2010>

Article 4 (Provision of Statistical Data)
Statistical data requested by the Minister of the Interior and Safety pursuant to Article 6-2 of the Act shall include the following matters: <Amended on Jan. 19, 2016; Nov. 29, 2016; Jul. 26, 2017>
1. Basic information on enterprises, such as trade names, business sectors, and numbers of employees;
2. Details of damage by cause and type of disaster;
3. The status of plan formulation, such as the scope of disaster mitigation activity plans;
4. The status of operation of disaster mitigation activity plans;
5. The actual status of support for excellent enterprises in disaster mitigation under Article 7 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "excellent enterprises").

CHAPTER III CERTIFICATION OF EXCELLENT DISASTER MITIGATION ENTERPRISES AND PERFORMANCE OF FUNCTIONS BY AGENTS

Article 5 (Evaluation Criteria for Excellent Enterprises)
(1) "Criteria prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 7 (3) of the Act shall be as follows: <Amended on Jan. 19, 2016; Nov. 29, 2016; Jul. 26, 2017>
1. The enterprise shall have an organization dedicated to disaster management;
2. The enterprise shall provide appropriate disaster management education to its workers, etc.;
3. The enterprise shall sufficiently provide for the costs of disaster mitigation activities under Article 26 of the Act;
4. The enterprise shall establish an appropriate cooperation framework for disaster prevention;
5. The enterprise shall conduct appropriate examination and analysis of disaster risks and vulnerabilities affecting its production facilities and workers, etc.;
6. The enterprise shall meet other evaluation criteria for excellent enterprises determined by the Minister of the Interior and Safety.
(2) Detailed evaluation criteria and methods under paragraph (1) shall be determined and publicly notified by the Minister of the Interior and Safety. <Amended on Jan. 19, 2016; Nov. 29, 2016; Jul. 26, 2017>

Article 6 Deleted. <Jul. 9, 2010>

Article 7 (Certification of Excellent Enterprises)
(1) Where the Minister of the Interior and Safety issues a certificate of excellent disaster mitigation enterprise to an excellent enterprise pursuant to Article 7 (3) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as an "excellent enterprise certificate"), the Minister shall publicly announce the following matters on the website of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety: <Amended on Jul. 9, 2010; Nov. 19, 2014; Jan. 19, 2016; Nov. 29, 2016; Jul. 26, 2017>
1. Name of the certification;
2. Trade name of the certified enterprise and name of its representative;
3. Certification number;
4. Date of certification and validity period.
(2) Where the Minister of the Interior and Safety designates a specialized institution to act on behalf of the Minister in certifying excellent enterprises under Article 8-2 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a "certification agency") for evaluation of disaster mitigation activities and issuance of excellent enterprise certificates under Article 7 (3) of the Act, the certification agency shall report to the Minister of the Interior and Safety the evaluation results and the fact that an excellent enterprise certificate has been issued. <Amended on Jul. 9, 2010; Jan. 19, 2016; Nov. 29, 2016; Jul. 26, 2017>
(3) Certification as an excellent enterprise under paragraphs (1) and (2) shall be valid for four years from the date of certification; provided, where important matters prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety change before expiry of the validity period, a new evaluation shall be undergone even before the certification validity period expires. <Amended on Dec. 31, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017; Feb. 18, 2025>
(4) The Minister of the Interior and Safety shall notify an enterprise certified as an excellent enterprise of the expiry of its excellent enterprise certification period 120 days before the certification validity period under paragraph (3) expires. <Amended on Jan. 19, 2016; Nov. 29, 2016; Jul. 26, 2017; Feb. 18, 2025>
(5) An enterprise intending to obtain an extension of the certification validity period under the main clause of paragraph (3) shall apply to the Minister of the Interior and Safety no later than 90 days before the certification validity period expires by submitting an application for extension of the certification validity period prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety, together with the following documents: <Added on Feb. 18, 2025>
1. A summary report on its disaster mitigation activity plan;
2. Documents substantiating matters corresponding to the evaluation criteria for excellent enterprises under Article 5 (1);
3. Documents substantiating its record of operating the disaster mitigation activity plan during the certification validity period under the main clause of paragraph (3).
(6) Upon receiving an application under paragraph (5), the Minister of the Interior and Safety shall examine the following matters and decide whether to extend the certification validity period: <Added on Feb. 18, 2025>
1. Whether the enterprise meets the evaluation criteria for excellent enterprises under Article 5 (1);
2. Its record of operating the disaster mitigation activity plan during the certification validity period under the main clause of paragraph (3).
(7) Where an enterprise issued an excellent enterprise certificate pursuant to paragraph (1) intends to have the certificate reissued due to a change of trade name or representative, damage to or loss of the excellent enterprise certificate, or similar reasons, the enterprise shall submit an application for reissuance prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety to the Minister of the Interior and Safety or a certification agency. <Amended on Dec. 31, 2008; Jul. 9, 2010; Mar. 23, 2013; Nov. 19, 2014; Jan. 19, 2016; Nov. 29, 2016; Jul. 26, 2017; Feb. 18, 2025>
(8) Other matters necessary for certification of excellent enterprises, procedures for issuance of excellent enterprise certificates, extension of certification validity periods, and related matters shall be determined and publicly notified by the Minister of the Interior and Safety. <Amended on Jan. 19, 2016; Nov. 29, 2016; Jul. 26, 2017; Feb. 18, 2025>

Article 8 (Revocation of Certification)
(1) Where certification as an excellent enterprise is revoked pursuant to Article 8 of the Act, the Minister of the Interior and Safety shall publish that fact in the Official Gazette. <Amended on Jul. 9, 2010; Jan. 19, 2016; Nov. 29, 2016; Jul. 26, 2017>
(2) An enterprise whose certification as an excellent enterprise has been revoked pursuant to Article 8 of the Act shall return its excellent enterprise certificate to the Minister of the Interior and Safety or a certification agency. <Amended on Jan. 19, 2016; Nov. 29, 2016; Jul. 26, 2017>
(3) The criteria for revocation of certification under Article 8 (2) of the Act shall be as specified in attached Table 1. <Added on Jul. 9, 2010>

Article 9 (Designation of Certification Agencies)
(1) A person intending to be designated as a certification agency pursuant to Article 8-2 of the Act shall have the organization, personnel, etc. necessary to perform certification functions on behalf of the Minister in accordance with the designation criteria specified in attached Table 2, and shall apply to the Minister of the Interior and Safety for designation. <Amended on Dec. 31, 2008; Jul. 9, 2010; Jan. 19, 2016; Nov. 29, 2016; Jul. 26, 2017>
(2) The Minister of the Interior and Safety may determine whether a person who applied for designation as a certification agency pursuant to paragraph (1) is suitable to perform certification functions on behalf of the Minister and designate that person as a certification agency. <Amended on Jan. 19, 2016; Nov. 29, 2016; Jul. 26, 2017>
(3) Other matters necessary for applications for designation and designation of certification agencies and related matters shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety. <Amended on Dec. 31, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>

Article 9-2 (Criteria for Revocation of Designation and Suspension of Functions of Certification Agencies)
(1) The criteria for revocation of designation and suspension of functions of certification agencies under Article 8-3 of the Act shall be as specified in attached Table 3.
(2) Where the Minister of the Interior and Safety revokes the designation of a certification agency or orders suspension of its functions pursuant to Article 8-3 of the Act, the Minister shall publish that fact in the Official Gazette. <Amended on Jan. 19, 2016; Nov. 29, 2016; Jul. 26, 2017>

Article 10 (Development of Specialized Personnel)
(1) The Minister of the Interior and Safety may operate specialized education courses under Article 10 (2) of the Act according to the following classifications: <Amended on Jan. 19, 2016; Nov. 29, 2016; Jul. 26, 2017>
1. The field of acting as an agent in formulating disaster mitigation activity plans;
2. The field of evaluation for certification of excellent enterprises;
3. Other specialized education courses in the field of disaster mitigation activities that the Minister of the Interior and Safety deems necessary.
(2) In operating specialized education courses under paragraph (1), the Minister of the Interior and Safety may exempt a person meeting requirements determined by the Minister of the Interior and Safety, such as having obtained a qualification in a disaster-related field before taking the specialized education course, from taking all or part of the course. <Amended on Apr. 29, 2014; Nov. 19, 2014; Jan. 19, 2016; Nov. 29, 2016; Jul. 26, 2017>
(3) Where the Minister of the Interior and Safety intends to collect costs incurred for education from persons completing education pursuant to Article 10 (4) of the Act (hereinafter referred to in this Article as "education costs"), the Minister shall take into account the hours of instruction, form of education, etc. for each course. <Amended on Jan. 19, 2016; Nov. 29, 2016; Jul. 26, 2017; Sep. 3, 2024>
(4) Where the Minister of the Interior and Safety intends to collect education costs from a person completing education pursuant to Article 10 (4) of the Act, and the enterprise to which the person belongs is a small business owner under Article 2 of the Framework Act on Small Business or a small or medium enterprise under Article 2 of the Framework Act on Small and Medium Enterprises, the Minister may reduce or exempt the education costs if the Minister deems a reduction of or exemption from education costs for the person completing education necessary to alleviate the enterprise's financial burden, support stable management, etc. <Added on Sep. 3, 2024>
(5) The Minister of the Interior and Safety may entrust functions concerning operation of the specialized education courses under paragraph (1) to an institution or organization with expertise in fields related to enterprises' disaster mitigation activities pursuant to Article 10 (2) of the Act. <Added on Jul. 9, 2010; Jan. 19, 2016; Nov. 29, 2016; Jul. 26, 2017>
(6) Where the Minister of the Interior and Safety entrusts functions pursuant to paragraph (5), the Minister shall publicly notify in the Official Gazette the name, representative, and location of the entrusted institution or organization and the details of the entrusted functions. <Added on Jul. 9, 2010; Jan. 19, 2016; Nov. 29, 2016; Jul. 26, 2017>
(7) Matters necessary for operation of specialized education courses, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) through (6), shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety. <Amended on Jul. 9, 2010; Mar. 23, 2013; Nov. 19, 2014; Jan. 19, 2016; Jul. 26, 2017>

Article 10-2 (Qualification Certification Examination)
(1) A person who has completed a specialized education course under Article 10 (1) (including a person exempted from taking the entire course pursuant to paragraph (2) of that Article) may take the examination administered by the Minister of the Interior and Safety pursuant to Article 10-2 (1) of the Act to certify qualification to manage disasters affecting enterprises (hereinafter referred to as the "qualification certification examination"). <Amended on Nov. 29, 2016; Jul. 26, 2017>
(2) The qualification certification examination shall be conducted for each specialized education course according to the classifications referred to in the subparagraphs of Article 10 (1). <Amended on Nov. 29, 2016>
(3) Subjects of the qualification certification examination shall be organized according to the instructional subjects of each specialized education course according to the classifications referred to in the subparagraphs of Article 10 (1), and details concerning examination subjects shall be determined and publicly notified by the Minister of the Interior and Safety. <Amended on Nov. 29, 2016; Jul. 26, 2017>
(4) A person who scores at least 40 points in each subject and an average of at least 60 points across all subjects, with a maximum of 100 points for each examination subject, shall pass the qualification certification examination. <Amended on Nov. 29, 2016>
(5) Where the Minister of the Interior and Safety intends to conduct a qualification certification examination, the Minister shall publicly announce the date, time, and place of the examination and other matters necessary for conducting the examination no later than 30 days before the examination date. <Amended on Nov. 29, 2016; Jul. 26, 2017>
(6) Matters necessary for conducting qualification certification examinations, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) through (5), shall be determined and publicly notified by the Minister of the Interior and Safety. <Amended on Nov. 29, 2016; Jul. 26, 2017>

Article 10-3 (Examination Fees)
(1) A person intending to take a qualification certification examination shall pay an examination fee. <Amended on Nov. 29, 2016>
(2) The Minister of the Interior and Safety shall refund the examination fee paid by a person under paragraph (1) according to the following classifications: <Amended on Jul. 26, 2017; Sep. 26, 2024>
1. Where an examination fee was erroneously paid or overpaid: The entire amount erroneously paid or overpaid;
2. Where the person is unable to take the examination for a reason attributable to the Minister of the Interior and Safety: The entire examination fee paid;
3. Where the person cancels the application during the period for receipt of examination applications: The entire examination fee paid;
4. Where the person is unable to take the examination due to hospitalization resulting from an accident or illness (limited to cases where the examination date falls within the hospitalization period): The entire examination fee paid;
5. Where the person is unable to take the examination because the person is subject to an order for medical examination, treatment, hospitalization, or isolation under the Infectious Disease Control and Prevention Act (limited to cases where the examination date falls within the period of medical examination, treatment, hospitalization, or isolation): The entire examination fee paid;
6. Where the person who paid the examination fee dies, or is unable to take the examination because any of the following persons dies during the period from seven days before the examination date through the examination date: The entire examination fee paid:
(a) The spouse of the person who paid the examination fee;
(b) Children of the person who paid the examination fee and of that person's spouse;
(c) Parents of the person who paid the examination fee and of that person's spouse;
(d) Paternal and maternal grandparents of the person who paid the examination fee and of that person's spouse;
(e) Siblings of the person who paid the examination fee and of that person's spouse.
(3) The amount of the examination fee under paragraph (1) shall be determined and publicly notified by the Minister of the Interior and Safety. <Amended on Jul. 26, 2017>

Article 10-4 (Issuance of Qualification Certificates)
(1) The Minister of the Interior and Safety shall issue a certificate of qualification to manage disasters affecting enterprises (hereinafter referred to as a "qualification certificate") to a person who has passed a qualification certification examination, for each field according to the classifications referred to in the subparagraphs of Article 10 (1). <Amended on Nov. 29, 2016; Jul. 26, 2017>
(2) Matters necessary for issuance of qualification certificates, other than those prescribed in paragraph (1), shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety. <Amended on Nov. 29, 2016; Jul. 26, 2017>

Article 10-5 (Entrustment of Administration of Qualification Certification Examinations)
(1) Pursuant to Article 10-2 (2) of the Act, the Minister of the Interior and Safety may entrust functions under paragraph (2) to an institution or organization satisfying all of the following requirements: <Amended on Nov. 29, 2016; Jul. 26, 2017>
1. It shall be a specialized institution or organization in the field of disasters established pursuant to the Civil Act or other Acts;
2. It shall have the organization, personnel, and facilities for administering qualification certification examinations;
3. It shall have expertise in enterprises' disaster mitigation activities;
4. It shall meet other requirements determined and publicly notified by the Minister of the Interior and Safety for smooth performance of functions concerning administration of qualification certification examinations and issuance of qualification certificates.
(2) An institution or organization under paragraph (1) shall perform the following functions concerning administration of qualification certification examinations and issuance of qualification certificates: <Amended on Nov. 29, 2016; Jul. 26, 2017>
1. Receipt of examination applications;
2. Receipt and refund of examination fees;
3. Preparation of examination questions and grading;
4. Administration and supervision of examinations;
5. Public announcement of successful candidates;
6. Issuance and reissuance of qualification certificates;
7. Other functions necessary for administration of qualification certification examinations and issuance of qualification certificates that are determined and publicly notified by the Minister of the Interior and Safety.
(3) Where the Minister of the Interior and Safety entrusts functions pursuant to paragraph (1), the Minister shall publicly notify in the Official Gazette the name, representative, and location of the entrusted institution or organization and the details of the entrusted functions. <Amended on Jul. 26, 2017>

Article 10-6 (Education for Corporate Disaster Managers)
(1) Pursuant to Article 10-3 of the Act, the Minister of the Interior and Safety shall conduct job training every three years to improve the capabilities of a person issued a qualification certificate (hereinafter referred to as a "corporate disaster manager") once one year has elapsed from the date on which the person was issued the qualification certificate. <Amended on Jan. 19, 2016; Nov. 29, 2016; Jul. 26, 2017>
(2) Matters necessary for the methods, content, etc. of job training for corporate disaster managers under paragraph (1) shall be determined and publicly notified by the Minister of the Interior and Safety. <Amended on Nov. 29, 2016; Jul. 26, 2017>

Article 10-7 (Criteria for Formulation of Disaster Mitigation Activity Plans)
(1) An enterprise intending to be certified as an excellent enterprise pursuant to Article 11 (1) of the Act shall formulate a disaster mitigation activity plan including the matters referred to in the subparagraphs of Article 5 (2) of the Act and submit it to the Minister of the Interior and Safety. <Amended on Jan. 19, 2016; Nov. 29, 2016; Jul. 26, 2017>
(2) Details concerning criteria for formulation of disaster mitigation activity plans under paragraph (1) and related matters shall be determined and publicly notified by the Minister of the Interior and Safety. <Amended on Jan. 19, 2016; Nov. 29, 2016; Jul. 26, 2017>

Article 11 (Registration Requirements for Plan Formulation Agents)
(1) A person intending to act as an agent in formulating disaster mitigation activity plans pursuant to Article 12 (1) of the Act shall secure specialized technical personnel under attached Table 4 and register the following matters with the Minister of the Interior and Safety: <Amended on Jul. 9, 2010; Nov. 19, 2014; Nov. 29, 2016; Jul. 26, 2017>
1. Name or trade name and location;
2. Status of securing technical personnel;
3. Other matters concerning registration determined and publicly notified by the Minister of the Interior and Safety.
(2) A person registered pursuant to paragraph (1) (hereinafter referred to as a "plan formulation agent") shall register changes pursuant to Article 12 (2) of the Act when any of the following matters changes: <Amended on Nov. 29, 2016>
1. Name or trade name and location of the plan formulation agent;
2. Status of securing technical personnel.

Article 12 (Criteria for Administrative Dispositions)
(1) The criteria for administrative dispositions against plan formulation agents under Article 17 (3) of the Act shall be as specified in attached Table 5. <Amended on Jul. 9, 2010>
(2) Where the Minister of the Interior and Safety revokes the registration of a plan formulation agent or imposes suspension of functions pursuant to paragraph (1), the Minister shall publish that fact in the Official Gazette. <Amended on Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>

CHAPTER IV SUPPORT FOR EXCELLENT ENTERPRISES

Article 13 (Discounts on Insurance Premiums)
(1) An institution operating disaster-related insurance for enterprises (hereinafter referred to as an "insurance operating institution") shall take into account the following matters where it intends to apply differentiated insurance premium rates pursuant to Article 20 (1) of the Act: <Amended on Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>
1. Matters concerning an enterprise's disaster mitigation activities for its facilities or workers, etc. in preparation for disaster risks;
2. The scale of investment in disaster prevention for disaster mitigation activities;
3. Other matters related to enterprises' disaster mitigation activities that are determined and publicly notified by the Minister of the Interior and Safety.
(2) When deciding whether to apply differentiated insurance premium rates pursuant to paragraph (1), an insurance operating institution may, where necessary, request materials necessary for its examination from the Minister of the Interior and Safety, a Special Metropolitan City Mayor, Metropolitan City Mayor, Special Self-Governing City Mayor, Do Governor, Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu (meaning the head of an autonomous Gu). <Amended on Jul. 9, 2010; Jan. 19, 2016; Nov. 29, 2016; Jul. 26, 2017>

Article 14 (Disaster Mitigation Equipment Funds)
"Other funds prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 23 (1) 3 of the Act means the following funds: <Amended on May 31, 2016; Sep. 29, 2016; Jan. 25, 2022>
1. The Credit Guarantee Fund under the Credit Guarantee Fund Act;
2. The Technology Guarantee Fund under the Technology Guarantee Fund Act;
3. Funds related to fostering local small and medium enterprises under Article 8 (1) 1 of the Act on Fostering and Promoting Innovation of Regional Small and Medium Enterprises.

CHAPTER V ESTABLISHMENT OF FOUNDATIONS FOR DISASTER MITIGATION ACTIVITIES

Article 15 (Implementation of Research and Development Projects for Disaster Mitigation Activities)
(1) The Minister of the Interior and Safety may enter into an agreement with an institution, organization, or business operator falling under any of the subparagraphs of Article 24 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a "research institution") and have it conduct a research and development project for disaster mitigation activities (hereinafter referred to as a "research and development project"). <Amended on Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>
(2) An agreement under paragraph (1) shall include the following matters:
1. Research tasks and persons responsible;
2. Costs necessary for the research and development project under Article 24 (2) of the Act and the method of payment;
3. Utilization of research and development project outcomes and collection of royalties therefor;
4. Matters concerning amendment or cancellation of the agreement and measures in the event of breach;
5. Other matters related to implementation of the research and development project.
(3) The head of a research institution that has entered into an agreement for a research and development project with the Minister of the Interior and Safety pursuant to paragraph (1) (hereinafter referred to as a "lead research and development institution") may entrust part of the research and development project to another research institution for performance. <Amended on Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017; Feb. 18, 2025>

Article 16 (Payment and Management of Contributions)
(1) Where costs necessary for a research and development project under Article 24 (2) of the Act include contributions from persons other than the Government or other enterprise research and development funds, a lead research and development institution shall enter into a contract concerning the contributions, etc. in advance with the persons bearing those costs. <Amended on Feb. 18, 2025>
(2) Government contributions under Article 24 (3) of the Act shall be paid in installments, taking into account the progress of the research and development project; provided, they may be paid in a lump sum where deemed necessary in consideration of the scale, commencement date, etc. of the research and development project.
(3) Where a lead research and development institution receives funds necessary for an enterprise's research and development project pursuant to Article 24 (2) of the Act, it shall establish a separate account to manage them. <Amended on Feb. 18, 2025>
(4) A lead research and development institution that receives contributions pursuant to paragraph (2) shall use those contributions only for the uses of research and development funds specified in attached Table 2 of the Enforcement Decree of the National Research and Development Innovation Act, and shall use them in accordance with the standards for use of research and development funds under Article 13 (4) of the National Research and Development Innovation Act. <Amended on Feb. 18, 2025>
(5) Where a lead research and development institution uses contributions received pursuant to paragraph (2) in violation of the uses and standards under paragraph (4) without justifiable grounds, the Minister of the Interior and Safety shall recover the relevant amount. <Amended on Feb. 18, 2025>

Article 17 (Provision for Costs of Disaster Mitigation Activities)
(1) An enterprise shall use funds provided for pursuant to Article 26 of the Act for the following purposes: <Amended on Dec. 31, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; Nov. 19, 2014; Nov. 29, 2016; Jul. 26, 2017>
1. Projects necessary for formulation and implementation of disaster mitigation activity plans;
2. Projects for urgently necessary repair and maintenance of facilities as a result of disaster mitigation activities;
3. Research and development projects necessary for preventing or reducing disasters;
4. Safety diagnosis and repair and reinforcement projects for enterprise facilities;
5. Emergency restoration and emergency measures for disaster-damaged facilities and stockpiling of disaster prevention supplies necessary therefor;
6. Education and publicity to improve the awareness of enterprise workers necessary for disaster mitigation activities;
7. Costs of employing corporate disaster managers;
8. Other projects prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety.
(2) An enterprise shall open an exclusive account at a financial institution, etc. to deposit and manage funds under paragraph (1), and shall operate and manage them so that they can be used smoothly for their intended purposes.

Article 18 (Support for Education and Training)
Where an enterprise provides education, training, etc. pursuant to Article 27 (1) of the Act, the Minister of the Interior and Safety may support the following matters where necessary: <Amended on Dec. 31, 2008; Jul. 9, 2010; Mar. 23, 2013; Nov. 19, 2014; Jan. 19, 2016; Nov. 29, 2016; Jul. 26, 2017>
1. Development and dissemination of education programs on disaster management standards and disaster mitigation activities;
2. Development and dissemination of lesson plans, teaching aids, and teaching materials necessary for operating the education referred to in subparagraph 1;
3. Identification and support of specialized instructors in corporate disaster management;
4. Other matters deemed necessary for education and training of enterprise workers, etc. for disaster mitigation and prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety.

CHAPTER VI SUPPLEMENTARY PROVISIONS

Article 19 (Registration of Incorporation of Corporate Disaster Mitigation Association)
The Corporate Disaster Mitigation Association under Article 30 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Association") shall register the following matters upon incorporation pursuant to paragraph (3) of that Article:
1. Name;
2. Purpose;
3. Location of its office;
4. Date of authorization for establishment;
5. Names and addresses of officers;
6. Total amount of assets.

Article 20 (Matters to Be Stated in Articles of Association of Association)
The Association shall state the following matters in its articles of association:
1. Name;
2. Purpose;
3. Location of its office;
4. Matters concerning business;
5. Matters concerning membership qualifications;
6. Matters concerning membership fees;
7. Matters concerning property and accounting;
8. Matters concerning officers and employees;
9. Matters concerning its structure and organization;
10. Matters concerning the general meeting and board of directors;
11. Matters concerning amendment of the articles of association.

Article 21 (Composition and Operation of Association)
(1) The officers of the Association shall consist of a president, vice presidents, directors, and auditors, and an auditor shall not concurrently serve as president, vice president, or director.
(2) The total number of officers of the Association shall not exceed 30, including one president, and the number of vice presidents shall not exceed five and the number of auditors shall not exceed two.
(3) The president and auditors shall be elected at a general meeting, and other officers shall be elected as prescribed by the articles of association.
(4) The term of office of officers of the Association shall be three years, and vice presidents, directors, and auditors may be reappointed for one consecutive term; provided, the term of an officer filling a vacancy shall be the remainder of the predecessor's term.
(5) The Association shall report the following matters to the Minister of the Interior and Safety in the manner prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety: <Amended on Dec. 31, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>
1. Important resolutions of the general meeting or board of directors;
2. Matters concerning the status of membership;
3. Other important matters related to operation of the Association and its members prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety.

Article 21-2 (Entrustment of Functions)
The Minister of the Interior and Safety may entrust the following functions to an institution or organization with expertise in fields related to enterprises' disaster mitigation activities pursuant to Article 33 of the Act: <Amended on Jan. 19, 2016; Nov. 29, 2016; Jul. 26, 2017>
1. Collection of education fees under Article 10 (4) of the Act;
2. Education for corporate disaster managers under Article 10-3 of the Act.

Article 22 (Evaluation and Awards)
The Minister of the Interior and Safety may formulate an annual evaluation plan for granting awards for disaster mitigation activities pursuant to Article 34 of the Act and regularly conduct evaluations and grant awards in accordance with the plan. <Amended on Jan. 19, 2016; Nov. 29, 2016; Jul. 26, 2017>

Article 23 (Processing of Personally Identifiable Information)
Where unavoidable for performing any of the following affairs, the Minister of the Interior and Safety (including persons entrusted with the functions of the Minister of the Interior and Safety pursuant to Article 10 or 10-2 of the Act) may process data containing resident registration numbers under subparagraph 1 of Article 19 of the Enforcement Decree of the Personal Information Protection Act: <Amended on Nov. 19, 2014; Jan. 19, 2016; Nov. 29, 2016; Jul. 26, 2017>
1. Affairs concerning development of specialized personnel, etc. under Article 10 of the Act;
2. Affairs concerning administration of qualification certification examinations and issuance of qualification certificates under Article 10-2 of the Act;
3. Affairs concerning confirmation of grounds for disqualification for registration of agents under Article 14 of the Act.

Article 24 Deleted. <Mar. 3, 2020>

ADDENDA <Presidential Decree No. 20556, Jan. 18, 2008>

Article 1 (Enforcement Date)
This Decree shall enter into force on January 20, 2008.

Article 2 (Transitional Measures concerning Registration Requirements for Plan Formulation Agents)
Notwithstanding Article 11 (1), a person registering as a plan formulation agent within one year from the date this Decree enters into force may register as a plan formulation agent on condition that, within one year after registration, the technical personnel held by that person complete the specialized education course in the field of acting as an agent in formulating disaster mitigation activity plans under Article 10 (1) 1 and are issued corporate disaster administrator certificates.

ADDENDA (Organization of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety and Its Affiliated Agencies) <Presidential Decree No. 21214, Dec. 31, 2008>

Article 1 (Enforcement Date)
This Decree shall enter into force on the date of its promulgation. <Proviso omitted>

Articles 2 through 4 Omitted.

Article 5 (Amendments to Other Statutes and Regulations)
